From d10b4c2476ae335f743f3775ff207a98918291ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Edmond <1571649+edmonddantes@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 10:09:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Drop completion packets that are not this context's ops

A handle passed to another process stays associated with the port of
the context that used it, so that process's own overlapped I/O on it
posts to this port, with an OVERLAPPED that is an address in the other
process. The dequeue, the completion pump and the teardown drain took
every packet's OVERLAPPED for one of their ops and wrote through it.

iocp_packet_op() now decides at all three. ior posts its own packets
with key 0, which no other process can. A packet with another key must
name an op slot of one of the context's chunks with a request in flight
(IOCP_OP_IO or IOCP_OP_IO_CANCEL), and each request takes one packet
(packet_taken, cleared as the request starts), so the pump cannot stage
an op twice. A foreign packet whose OVERLAPPED lands exactly on a slot
with a request in flight is still taken for it, and that request's own
packet then for the slot's next request: nothing in a packet tells them
apart. The key is not compared: an op on a duplicate handle takes the
association over under its own key, and later ops on the first handle
complete under that key.

A dropped packet is not counted. A dequeue that drops one waits again
with the time left; after 64 drops it returns -EAGAIN, so a peek
returns however fast such packets come. The chunk list is published
with release, as the walk takes no lock while the poller thread may grow
the pool. The README says that
Windows completes such foreign I/O only when the ring dequeues its
packet, unless its event has the low-order bit set.

test_iocp_backend: overlapped reads issued outside ior on the ring's
pipe handle stand in for the other process. At each of the three sites
their packets are dropped, the reads complete and the memory past
their OVERLAPPED stays as it was; one 0 ms peek finds the ring's own
write behind three of them, and a peek gives up after 64 of 70. Ops
taking turns on a pipe handle and its duplicate all complete.
